0105_sync_blocked_reason_clock_off — NOT CARRIED AS A STEP, FOR `0102.noop`'S REASON VERBATIM.

The shared entry widens the closed set on `mailboxes.sync_blocked_reason` by one more member,
'clock_off', the way Postgres allows: DROP CONSTRAINT then ADD CONSTRAINT. SQLite has neither. A
CHECK is part of the table definition there, so changing one member means rebuilding a fifty-column
table by hand for a one-word widening.

It does not need to be a step. This journal has never shipped: nothing outside a test's temporary
file has ever run `0001_baseline.sql`, so there is no store in the world holding the four-member
constraint that a migration would have to correct. The member goes in the BASELINE, which is where
the current closed set belongs while the baseline is still unreleased, and both twins are checked
against `MAILBOX_SYNC_BLOCK_REASONS` itself — `sqlite-baseline.test.ts` stores every member in a
real store and refuses one outside the set, and `schema-twin-parity.test.ts` compares the two
twins' CHECK text.

WHAT CHANGES THE DAY THIS JOURNAL SHIPS is what `0102.noop` and `0104.noop` already say, and the
same answer will serve all three.
